WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:03.060
This episode has been supported by Lambda
Labs.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.049
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:13.320
In our earlier paper, Gaussian Material Synthesis,
we made a neural renderer, and what this neural

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:19.580
renderer was able to do is reproduce the results
of a light transport simulation within 4 to

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.789
6 milliseconds in a way that is almost pixel
perfect.

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:29.740
It took a fixed camera and scene, and we were
able to come up with a ton of different materials,

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.760
and it was always able to guess what the output
would look like if we changed the physical

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.960
properties of a material.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.710
This is a perfect setup for material synthesis
where these restrictions are not too limiting.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.410
Trying to perform high-quality neural rendering
has been a really important research problem

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:52.219
lately, and everyone is asking the question,
can we do more with this?

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:56.609
Can we move around with the camera and have
a neural network predict what the scene would

00:00:56.609 --> 00:00:57.710
look like?

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.489
Can we do this with animations?

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:04.470
Well, have a look at this new paper which
is a collaboration between researchers at

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:10.110
the Technical University of Munich and Stanford
University, where all we need is some video

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.830
footage of a person or object.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:18.350
It takes a close look at this kind of information,
and can offer three killer applications.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:23.440
One, it can synthesize the object from new
viewpoints.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:30.950
Two, it can create a video of this scene and
imagine what it would look like if we reorganized

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:53.970
it, or can even add more objects to it.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:58.970
And three, perhaps everyone’s favorite,
performing facial reenactment from a source

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.000
to a target actor.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.710
As with many other methods, these neural textures
are stored on top of the 3D objects, however,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:13.150
a more detailed, high-dimensional description
is also stored and learned by this algorithm,

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:18.329
which enables it to have a deeper understanding
of intricate light transport effects create

00:02:18.329 --> 00:02:19.829
these new views.

00:02:19.829 --> 00:02:25.110
For instance, it is particularly good at reproducing
specular highlights, which typically change

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.890
rapidly as we change our viewpoint for the
object.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:33.000
One of the main challenges was building a
learning algorithm that can deal with this

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.610
kind of complexity.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:39.430
The synthesis of mouth movements and teeth
was always the achilles heel of these methods,

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.110
so have a look at how well this one does with
it!

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:46.689
You can also see with the comparisons here
that in general, this new technique smokes

00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:48.269
the competition.

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:51.349
So how much training data do we need to achieve
this?

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:56.250
I would imagine that this would take hours
and hours of video footage, right?

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.109
No, not at all.

00:02:58.109 --> 00:03:02.810
This is what the results look like as a function
of the amount of training data.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:09.469
On the left, you see that is already kinda
works with 125 images, but contains artifacts,

00:03:09.469 --> 00:03:13.769
but if we can supply a 1000 images, we’re
good.

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:18.659
Note that a 1000 images sounds like a lot,
but it really isn’t, it’s just half a

00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:20.760
minute worth of video.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.849
How crazy is that?

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:27.389
Some limitations still apply, you see one
failure case right here, and the neural network

00:03:27.389 --> 00:03:32.799
typically needs to be retrained if we wish
to use it on new objects, but this work finally

00:03:32.799 --> 00:03:38.879
generalizes to multiple viewpoints, animation,
scene editing, lots of different materials

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:43.909
and geometries, and I can only imagine what
we’ll get two more papers down the line.

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:48.329
Respect to Justus for accomplishing this,
and in general, make sure to have a look at

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:54.909
Matthias Niessner’s lab, who just got tenured
as a full professor and he’s only 32 years

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:55.909
old.

00:03:55.909 --> 00:03:56.909
Congratulations!

